# Git workflow (light GitFlow)

This repository uses a simplified GitFlow: one protected mainline plus short-lived topic branches.

## Branches

- `master`: production-ready; always deployable. Do not commit directly.
- `feature/<short-description>`: new work (e.g. `feature/chat-reconnect`)
- `fix/<short-description>`: bugfixes (e.g. `fix/splash-handoff`)

Rules:

- Branch from `master`; merge back via pull request with review and passing CI.
- Keep topic branches short-lived (days, not weeks); rebase or merge `master` often to avoid drift.
- One logical change per branch; use kebab-case, lowercase names.
- Tag releases on `master` with semantic versions when shipping (e.g. `v0.2.0`).

This repo does not use `develop`, `release/*`, or `hotfix/*`. Urgent production fixes follow the same `fix/*` flow from `master`.

## Commit messages (Source Control "Generate commit message")

When Cursor auto-generates a Git commit message for this repository:

- Use Conventional Commits: `type(optional-scope): short summary`
- Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`, `test`, `ci`, `style`, `perf`
- Subject: imperative mood ("Add", "Fix", "Update"), max ~72 characters, no trailing period
- Focus on WHY / intent of the change, not a file-by-file changelog
- One subject line only unless a short body is truly needed
- English is preferred for commit subjects
- Do NOT use verbose essay style (multi-sentence summaries listing every touched file)
- Do NOT start with "Update filename:" as the whole message pattern
- Branch names are separate from commit messages; do not paste the branch name into the subject

Examples:

- Good: `feat(chat): retry unsent messages after reconnect`
- Good: `fix(splash): dismiss overlay when bootstrap completes`
- Good: `docs: add local relay setup instructions`
- Good: `ci: run lint and typecheck on pull requests`
- Bad: `Update README.md: Revise architecture section and document setup steps for development environment...`
- Bad: `feature/chat-reconnect: add retry logic`
